## Usage

### Java

```java
PowerOfTwoMaxHeap heap = new PowerOfTwoMaxHeap(2); // 4 children per node
heap.insert(5);
heap.insert(42);
heap.insert(7);

heap.peekMax(); // 42
heap.popMax();  // 42
heap.size();    // 2
```

An initial capacity can be passed as a second argument: `new PowerOfTwoMaxHeap(2, 1_000_000)`.

### JavaScript

```js
const { PowerOfTwoMaxHeap } = require('./PowerOfTwoMaxHeap');

const heap = new PowerOfTwoMaxHeap(2); // 4 children per node
heap.insert(5);
heap.insert(42);
heap.insert(7);

heap.peekMax(); // 42
heap.popMax();  // 42
heap.size;      // 2
```

## API

| Operation | Java | JavaScript | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| Add a value | `insert(int)` | `insert(number)` | O(log<sub>d</sub> n) |
| Remove and return the largest | `popMax()` | `popMax()` | O(d log<sub>d</sub> n) |
| Return the largest | `peekMax()` | `peekMax()` | O(1) |
| Number of values | `size()` | `size` | O(1) |
| Whether the heap is empty | `isEmpty()` | `isEmpty` | O(1) |
| Children per node | `branchingFactor()` | `branchingFactor` | O(1) |

Here d = 2^`childExponent` and n is the number of values. A larger d makes the heap shallower, which speeds up `insert` and makes `popMax` scan more children per level.

## Behavior and limits

- `childExponent` must be an integer from 0 to 30. Anything else throws `IllegalArgumentException` (Java) or `RangeError` (JavaScript). The upper limit keeps 2^`childExponent` within a 32-bit int.
- `popMax` and `peekMax` on an empty heap throw `NoSuchElementException` (Java) or `Error` (JavaScript).
- The JavaScript version rejects `NaN` and non-number values with a `TypeError`.
- Child indexes are computed in `long` (Java) or double (JavaScript) arithmetic. A 32-bit `i << childExponent` overflows at large exponents even for a small heap.
- When 2^`childExponent` is larger than the number of values, the root has every other value as a child, so `popMax` is a linear scan.
- With `childExponent = 0` every operation is O(n).
- The backing array doubles when full and is capped just below the platform array limit. It does not shrink after pops.

## Tests

```
# Java (JDK 11 or later)
javac PowerOfTwoMaxHeap.java PowerOfTwoMaxHeapTest.java
java PowerOfTwoMaxHeapTest

# JavaScript (Node 18 or later)
node PowerOfTwoMaxHeap.test.js
```

Both suites check every exponent from 0 to 30 against a reference implementation (`PriorityQueue` in Java, a sorted array in JavaScript). They use mixed insert and pop sequences, heavy duplicates, ascending and descending input, extreme values, invalid arguments, and empty-heap errors. Each also prints a rough timing for 2 million random values at several exponents.
